@@ -0,0 +1,349 @@
+# Deployment-wide AI Gateway custom-provider models
+
+## Status
+
+Proposed implementation plan. No implementation has been made from this document.
+
+## Goal
+
+Let a deployment administrator publish a catalog of models served by **custom providers that
+are already configured on the deployment's Cloudflare AI Gateway**. Every user can select those
+models in Workshop chats and workspaces. The Workshop must send their requests through that
+deployment gateway using the gateway's preconfigured provider credentials.
+
+This is deliberately unrelated to Workers AI. It is also not a UI for creating or credentialing
+an AI Gateway custom provider.
+
+## Product decisions already made
+
+- An administrator creates, edits, and deletes the catalog entries in the Workshop admin UI.
+- An entry has a human display name, a custom-provider slug, and a provider model ID.
+- The initial UI may require manual entry of the slug and model ID; model discovery is out of
+  scope.
+- Every user of the deployment sees and can select every enabled catalog entry.
+- Only administrators may mutate the shared catalog.
+- The custom-provider protocol is OpenAI-compatible for this first version.
+- The custom provider and its credentials are configured outside Workshop, in Cloudflare AI
+  Gateway. No upstream provider credentials, gateway credentials, or custom-provider setup fields
+  are entered or stored through this feature.
+- Existing user-owned direct/BYOK models remain unchanged. Do not add a new user-owned-model
+  feature as part of this work.
+- The first version supports custom providers only. Supporting arbitrary built-in AI Gateway
+  providers is a later extension.
+- **Proposed policy — needs confirmation:** a shared custom-provider model always uses the
+  deployment gateway and is billed there, even if its user has connected a personal Cloudflare
+  account for unified billing. A custom provider is account-specific and normally does not exist in
+  that user's `default` gateway.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Creating, editing, deleting, or storing Cloudflare AI Gateway provider configuration.
+- Discovering models from the gateway or from an upstream provider.
+- Supporting arbitrary native upstream request formats.
+- Changing the existing Workers AI configuration or treating a custom provider as Workers AI.
+- Replacing the current direct-provider model picker.
+
+## Existing implementation and constraints
+
+### The current “AI providers” page is a per-user model picker
+
+`packages/workshop-frontend/src/routes/providers.tsx` calls
+`AuthenticatedApi.listModels()`, `getQuickModel()`, and `getAiConfig()`, then renders
+`AddModelModal`.
+
+`packages/workshop-frontend/src/AddModelModal.tsx` only offers provider types defined by
+`AiModelProvider` and `SUGGESTED_MODELS`. In direct mode it stores credentials in the user's
+model configuration. In platform AI Gateway mode it hides credential fields and saves an empty
+token because gateway routing ignores per-model credentials.
+
+The current per-user storage is `UserDurableObject.storage.aiModels`, keyed by the raw model ID
+(`packages/workshop-backend/src/user.ts`). That is unsuitable as the identifier for the new shared
+catalog: raw model IDs are not globally unique across providers or endpoints.
+
+### The current gateway path is a fixed adapter set
+
+`AiModelProvider` in `packages/workshop-shared/src/api.ts` is a fixed union:
+`openai`, `anthropic`, `google`, `cloudflare`, and `ollama`.
+
+`packages/workshop-backend/src/ai-models.ts` explicitly builds the API descriptor and gateway path
+for each of those types. `gatewayNativeModel()` does not have a generic custom-provider branch.
+The `AiGatewayConfig` provider set in `packages/workshop-backend/src/ai-gateway.ts` only produces
+suggested models for those known types.
+
+This feature therefore needs a distinct configuration/routing variant; it cannot be implemented by
+placing a custom-provider slug in the existing `provider` field and hoping that the current switch
+will route it.
+
+### Deployment-wide admin configuration is the correct persistence mechanism
+
+`AdminSettings` owns deployment-wide `AdminConfig`, serializes it to the reserved admin-config KV
+record, and `readAdminConfig()` reads the mirrored record cheaply on runtime paths:
+
+- configuration schema and backwards-compatible parsing:
+  `packages/workshop-backend/src/admin-config.ts`;
+- authoritative mutation and KV mirror:
+  `packages/workshop-backend/src/admin-settings.ts`;
+- admin capability: `AdminApi` in `packages/workshop-shared/src/api.ts`, implemented by
+  `AdminApiImpl` in `admin-settings.ts`;
+- existing admin UI: `packages/workshop-frontend/src/AdminPage.tsx`.
+
+The feature must use that mechanism rather than a new Durable Object, KV key, service, or a
+per-user model record. The admin capability is already minted only for admins by
+`AuthenticatedApi.getAdminApi()`.
+
+## Proposed data model
+
+Add a deployment-owned entry, conceptually:
+
+```ts
+type AdminAiGatewayCustomModel = {
+  // Stable opaque identifier owned by Workshop, not the provider's model ID.
+  id: string;
+
+  // Text displayed to users.
+  displayName: string;
+
+  // The already-configured Cloudflare AI Gateway custom-provider slug.
+  providerSlug: string;
+
+  // The upstream model identifier sent through that provider.
+  modelId: string;
+
+  // Relative OpenAI Chat Completions endpoint path, e.g. "v1/chat/completions" or
+  // "v4/chat/completions". This is appended to the provider's Cloudflare-configured base URL.
+  chatCompletionsPath: string;
+};
+```
+
+Add an ordered `aiGatewayCustomModels` collection to `AdminConfig`, defaulting to `[]`. Persist
+and parse it defensively, just as other AdminConfig fields are parsed. Older stored config must
+continue to load as an empty catalog.
+
+The opaque `id` is required because a raw model ID may collide with:
+
+- another custom provider's model;
+- a user-owned direct/BYOK model; or
+- a model currently synthesized from `SUGGESTED_MODELS`.
+
+Chat selection and storage must use the opaque ID. The raw `modelId` is only upstream routing data.
+Do not overload `AiChatAuthorInfo.id` with the raw provider model identifier for the new entries.
+
+Use a reserved, non-user-model namespace such as `aig-custom:<uuid>`, generated only by the
+backend. `addModel()` must reject that namespace, and shared-model resolution must happen before
+per-user resolution. Otherwise a user could create a model with the same ID and a deleted shared
+entry could silently begin using their personal model.
+
+Represent the resolved entry as a separate discriminated `AiModelConfig` variant rather than an
+arbitrary value in `AiModelProvider`. For example, its provider discriminator can be
+`"ai-gateway-custom"` and it can carry `providerSlug`, the raw upstream `model`, its relative
+Chat Completions path. Keeping it out of `AiModelProvider` prevents `AddModelModal`,
+`SUGGESTED_MODELS`, and
+`CF_AI_GATEWAY_PROVIDERS` from treating it as a user-configurable native provider.
+
+## Required API and UI work
+
+1. Extend the shared, documented public API with an admin view/input type and AdminApi methods to
+   create, update, and delete entries. The server generates IDs; clients never choose them.
+2. Include the catalog in `AdminSettingsView` so the existing admin settings load remains a single
+   RPC call. Add its reserved IDs to the enabled `AiGatewayInfo` response so the ordinary providers
+   page can render them as deployment-managed without inspecting an opaque-ID convention.
+3. Add an **AI Gateway Models** section or tab to `AdminPage` with create, edit, and delete
+   controls. It needs display-name, provider-slug, model-ID, and Chat Completions endpoint-path
+   inputs (for example `v1/chat/completions` or `v4/chat/completions`). It must clearly say that
+   credentials are configured in Cloudflare AI Gateway.
+4. Merge the shared catalog into ordinary users' `listModels()` results, while retaining their
+   current personal-model results. Only expose it while the deployment gateway is configured.
+5. Resolve a selected catalog ID to the deployment-owned configuration in `getChatContext()`,
+   `setPreferredModel()`, and every path that obtains a chat context (chat start/resume, external
+   messages, callbacks, model bindings, and attachment validation). A user must never receive the
+   deployment gateway token or the admin configuration capability.
+6. Show shared models as deployment-managed in the regular provider/model page. Regular users
+   cannot edit or delete them; `deleteModel()` must reject their reserved IDs. Admins manage them
+   only from the admin page, avoiding two competing ownership models.
+7. Preserve clear behavior for deleted catalog entries referenced by an existing chat: selection
+   must fail as an unavailable model rather than silently routing to a different model.
+
+## Routing requirements
+
+Add a specific model-config variant for a deployment AI Gateway custom provider. Its routing data
+must include the opaque catalog ID, the custom-provider slug, and the upstream model ID.
+
+The backend must construct the request using the custom provider's OpenAI-compatible route on the
+**deployment** gateway. It must not:
+
+- route through the Workers AI endpoint;
+- use a per-user API URL or token;
+- send a request directly to the upstream provider; or
+- use the current hard-coded `cloudflare` provider branch.
+
+Reuse the deployment gateway's authenticated request and gateway-log-cost path where applicable.
+The gateway account ID and Run/Read token remain environment secrets and never cross the RPC
+boundary.
+
+### Confirmed billing/routing policy
+
+Today `getModel()` prioritizes a connected user's own Cloudflare AI Gateway (unified billing)
+before the platform gateway (`packages/workshop-backend/src/ai-models.ts`). A deployment custom
+provider normally does not exist in that user's personal/default gateway.
+
+The implementation recognizes the `ai-gateway-custom` config **before** that generic user-gateway
+precedence and routes it only through the deployment gateway. This prevents an identically named
+model from reaching a different provider and means these calls are billed to the deployment
+gateway. Requiring every user to configure the same custom provider in their personal account is
+not in scope.
+
+## Validation and security requirements
+
+- Allow catalog mutation only through the admin capability. Do not trust the visibility of the
+  admin React route as authorization.
+- Reject mutations unless platform AI Gateway mode is configured; otherwise entries cannot run.
+- Validate maximum lengths, non-empty trimmed values, and uniqueness of the opaque ID. Define
+  explicit duplicate behavior for `(providerSlug, modelId)`—prefer rejecting duplicates.
+- Validate the slug as Cloudflare's documented alphanumeric-and-hyphen custom-provider slug and
+  reject controls/empty values. It becomes part of the gateway's provider-specific URL path.
+- Require a normalized relative endpoint path that ends exactly in `chat/completions`; reject an
+  absolute URL, query/fragment, empty, `.`/`..`, encoded slash, and control characters. Encode
+  every accepted dynamic path segment when building the gateway URL, so the configured custom
+  provider is the only possible upstream authority.
+- Bound display names and model IDs, reject control characters, and reject duplicate
+  `(providerSlug, modelId, chatCompletionsPath)` entries.
+- Keep `apiToken`, account ID, gateway token, headers, request bodies, and model prompts out of
+  this catalog and out of logs.
+- Continue to distinguish deployment-managed models from user-owned direct models for edit/delete
+  authorization.
+
+## Verified Cloudflare request contract and endpoint mapping
+
+Cloudflare's current [Custom Providers documentation](https://developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/configuration/custom-providers/)
+provides two routes. Its Unified `/compat` API fixes the upstream endpoint to
+`/chat/completions`, so it cannot meet providers whose OpenAI-compatible endpoint is, for example,
+`/v4/chat/completions`.
+
+Use Cloudflare's **provider-specific endpoint** instead:
+
+```text
+Gateway base URL: https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/{account}/{gateway}/custom-{slug}/{path-prefix}
+pi appends:       /chat/completions
+Upstream URL:     {custom-provider-base-url}/{path-prefix}/chat/completions
+```
+
+The admin enters the complete relative endpoint path (`v1/chat/completions`,
+`v4/chat/completions`, or another safe path ending in `chat/completions`). The backend validates
+that fixed suffix and derives the gateway base URL from the preceding path prefix. It then uses
+pi's `openai-completions` adapter, which appends the fixed suffix, and sends the raw upstream model
+ID in the JSON `model` field.
+
+The deployment's existing `cf-aig-authorization` header and null `Authorization` / `x-api-key`
+suppressions are the intended stored-key/BYOK path. Do not send an upstream provider key from
+Workshop. The gateway log route remains the deployment's named gateway, so the existing cost-log
+lookup should be retained and covered by an integration smoke test.
+
+### Day-one PDF support
+
+Use the existing OpenAI Chat Completions data-URL payload for every custom model: pi represents a
+staged PDF as an `image_url` data URL with MIME `application/pdf`, and Workshop must permit that
+upload and replay it unchanged. This is the simplest dependency-free day-one path and follows the
+same OpenAI-Completions request shape as images.
+
+PDF input is not portable across every implementation that calls itself OpenAI-compatible. A
+provider that rejects this payload will surface its normal request error; Workshop will not add a
+provider-specific file-ID/Responses adapter, PDF-to-image renderer, or PDF text-extraction
+dependency in this first version. Add one only when a concrete provider requires it.
+
+The generic protocol also cannot establish an individual model's token window. Use the existing
+unknown-model defaults (128K context and 4K output) initially; a future catalog can add validated
+per-model limits if needed.
+
+## Implementation sequence
+
+1. **Shared schema and persisted catalog**
+   - In `packages/workshop-shared/src/api.ts`, add fully documented catalog view/input types,
+     the discriminated custom-model config variant, AdminApi CRUD methods, and
+     `AiGatewayInfo.deploymentModelIds`.
+   - In `packages/workshop-backend/src/admin-config.ts`, add the catalog to `AdminConfig`, a
+     default, parser/sanitizer, reserved-ID helpers, and pure helpers that turn an entry into its
+     user-visible profile and resolved config. Normalize the persisted Chat Completions endpoint
+     path and drop malformed/legacy entries.
+   - In `packages/workshop-backend/src/admin-settings.ts`, add serialized CRUD methods that mutate
+     the single admin-config record and mirror it to KV. Generate IDs here; reject mutations when
+     the deployment gateway configuration is absent or invalid.
+   - Update `AdminSettingsView` assembly and add focused `admin-config` tests. Keep the backend
+     diff small: no new storage binding, Durable Object, or environment variable is needed.
+
+2. **Resolve shared entries without changing personal models**
+   - In `packages/workshop-backend/src/user.ts`, read the mirrored admin config for `listModels()`,
+     `setPreferredModel()`, and `getChatContext()`. Merge only valid shared entries when gateway
+     mode is enabled; resolve them before `storage.aiModels`.
+   - Make `addModel()` and `deleteModel()` reject the reserved shared-ID namespace. This protects
+     deletion/resume semantics even after an admin removes an entry.
+   - In `packages/workshop-backend/src/server.ts`, make `getAiConfig()` return the shared IDs along
+     with the normal enabled-provider information. Do not expose slugs, upstream IDs, or tokens
+     through that response.
+   - Update dependent exhaustive code for the new config discriminator, notably
+     `agent-compaction.ts`, `chat-attachment-validation.ts`, `agent.ts`, and
+     `chat-attachment-pdf.ts`. Mark the custom OpenAI-Completions handle as PDF-capable so upload
+     validation and replay send its data-URL payload unchanged. Use conservative unknown-model
+     limits; do not add a custom-provider option to `AddModelModal`.
+
+3. **Add the deployment-only routing branch**
+   - In `packages/workshop-backend/src/ai-models.ts`, identify the custom config before the
+     connected-user gateway branch. Require `getAiGatewayConfig(env)` and route only through its
+     deployment gateway.
+   - Synthesize an `openai-completions` model with base URL
+     `https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/{account}/{gateway}/custom-{providerSlug}/{path-prefix}`
+     and the raw upstream `modelId`. Derive and path-encode `path-prefix` from the validated
+     endpoint path. Reuse deployment gateway authorization, metadata, and log route; do not add
+     the custom slug to `CF_AI_GATEWAY_PROVIDERS`.
+   - Ensure a forged custom config cannot fall through to direct-provider routing or a user's
+     default gateway. Add descriptor and captured-request tests in `ai-models.test.ts` for the
+     exact URL, request `model`, headers, log route, and personal-gateway precedence.
+
+4. **Admin and regular-user UI**
+   - Add a compact AI Gateway Models panel/tab to `packages/workshop-frontend/src/AdminPage.tsx`.
+     It loads from `AdminSettingsView`, manages local form/error/busy state, and refreshes settings
+     after mutation. Follow the existing RPC-stub lifecycle pattern.
+   - Update `packages/workshop-frontend/src/routes/providers.tsx` to use
+     `deploymentModelIds`: label those rows “deployment-managed” and omit their delete action.
+     Keep regular Add Model behavior unchanged.
+   - Exercise onboarding as well: it already obtains `listModels()`, so shared models must be
+     selectable there without exposing an edit path.
+
+5. **Validate end to end**
+   - Run the focused backend tests first, then `pnpm --filter @gadgets/workshop-backend test` (or
+     the package's established test command), `pnpm build`, and `pnpm lint` before review.
+   - Finally, send a real non-production request through a configured custom provider with only
+     `cf-aig-authorization` present. Verify the provider's stored/default key is used and that a
+     gateway log with a retrievable cost is produced.
+
+## Test plan
+
+- Admin authorization: non-admin callers cannot obtain the admin capability or mutate the catalog.
+- Config persistence: defaults, malformed/legacy config, create/edit/delete, and KV-mirror failure
+  behavior.
+- Validation: blank, oversized, malformed, duplicate, and path-special slug/model/endpoint values.
+- Visibility: every user lists shared models; personal direct/BYOK models remain visible only to
+  their owner; deleted shared entries disappear.
+- Identity: same raw model ID can coexist across two custom providers and with a personal model;
+  user model creation cannot claim the reserved shared-ID namespace.
+- Resolution: opaque shared IDs resolve to the intended slug/model; stale chat IDs fail clearly and
+  never fall through to a personal model.
+- Routing: a shared custom model uses the deployment gateway route and credentials, never Workers
+  AI or a direct endpoint; verify `v1` and `v4` endpoint paths, encoded path segments, request API
+  shape, headers, and gateway log route.
+- PDFs: custom models accept, replay, and send the expected `application/pdf` data URL through
+  Chat Completions. A rejecting provider surfaces its response error; no alternative PDF dialect is
+  silently substituted.
+- Billing precedence: cover the policy selected above for a user with a connected personal
+  Cloudflare account.
+- Frontend: admin CRUD states/errors and regular-user read-only presentation.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- An admin can create, edit, and delete a deployment-wide custom-provider model entry.
+- A non-admin cannot mutate it through the UI or a crafted RPC call.
+- Every user can select the entry without entering an upstream API key.
+- The request reaches the configured deployment AI Gateway custom provider using its
+  OpenAI-compatible contract, not Workers AI.
+- Two entries can use identical upstream model IDs when their provider slugs differ.
+- Existing per-user direct/BYOK models continue to work unchanged.
+- Removing an entry does not cause an old chat to select or execute a different model.
